      "name": "history-10-typed",
      "question": "Why?",
      "recognizedQuestion": "Why?",
      "answer": "Because a warp is the GPU’s instruction-scheduling group: its 32 threads share an instruction stream. When threads take different branches, the GPU executes the different paths with masks enabling only the relevant threads, which can reduce efficiency. Modern NVIDIA GPUs support independent thread scheduling, so you shouldn’t assume all warp threads always execute in lockstep.",
